[manpage_begin nssmtpd n 2.8]
[moddesc {NaviServer External Modules}]
[titledesc {SMTP reception, authenticated submission and upstream relay}]
[description]
The nssmtpd module supplies SMTP service and Tcl callbacks for receiving mail,
resolving envelope recipients and forwarding through an upstream mail server.
It also supplies outbound SMTP sending support. Keep an upstream MTA for persistent
queueing and delivery retries. The module does not provide a general local mailbox store.

[section INSTALLATION]
Build and install the module with the shared NaviServer Makefile support, then load
it in the chosen virtual server and configure its listener and Tcl callbacks.
Use a dedicated submission listener and TLS/authentication as appropriate for the
installation. Relay and local-recipient policies determine which destinations are
accepted; do not expose unrestricted relaying.

[section MAIL FLOW]
Incoming SMTP sessions pass through recipient resolution and recipient policy.
Accepted message data can be forwarded through the configured upstream relay.
Alias expansion preserves the envelope semantics and does not rewrite message headers.
Recipient-policy findings are available to DATA policies, including greylisting findings.
SMTP event and send logs provide session and transaction context for administration.
For ports, relay paths, policies and operational examples, see
[uri https://github.com/naviserver-project/nssmtpd/blob/main/README.md {the repository guide}]
and its mail-flow diagram. Older external scanner integrations are described as legacy support.

[section QUARANTINE]
Enable [const quarantinePolicyRejects] to hold all DATA-policy rejections for manual
inspection. Configure an absolute persistent [const quarantineDirectory], with
[const quarantineRetention], [const quarantineMaxBytes] and [const quarantineMaxMessages].
The default initializer creates a private spool; custom initializers must call
[cmd smtpd::quarantineinit]. Each entry contains a binary message.eml and a UTF-8
metadata.tcl dictionary, read as data rather than sourced.

A durable hold returns SMTP 250 without forwarding. Storage/capacity failures
return 451. Recipient-policy rejections and DATA deferrals are unaffected.
The directory must have one owning process and support atomic rename and fsync.
Use a separate persistent directory for every virtual server.

[cmd {smtpd::quarantine status}] reports limits and retained states.
[cmd {smtpd::quarantine list}] accepts -limit and -offset and returns entries,
nextOffset and more. [cmd {smtpd::quarantine show}] takes an ID and returns metadata
and the message path; [cmd {smtpd::quarantine message}] returns the bytes.
[cmd {smtpd::quarantine release}] sends the recorded recipients without re-expanding
aliases or reapplying the incoming policy. Delivered recipients are not resent.
[cmd {smtpd::quarantine delete}] removes an inactive entry.
[cmd {smtpd::quarantine cleanup}] expires old held/released entries; cleanup also
runs hourly and on admission. Expiry discards held mail without a bounce.

Failed deliveries remain uncertain and are excluded from expiry. After checking
upstream logs, [cmd {smtpd::quarantine retry}] takes an ID, recipient and -confirm
to permit a retry, which may duplicate upstream acceptance. Interrupted releases
remain locked until a later process runs [cmd {smtpd::quarantine recover}] with the
ID. Never automatically retry an uncertain SMTP outcome. Unpublished .pending-*
directories require manual inspection after crashes. Capacity limits are admission
limits rather than filesystem quotas; allow space for temporary files and metadata.

The low-level [cmd {ns_smtpd getmessage}] returns unstuffed binary message data;
[cmd {ns_smtpd sendresolved}] accepts sender, a one-recipient list and a binary data
variable and bypasses aliases. [cmd {ns_smtpd filesync}] syncs a file or directory.
See the repository guide for nscp examples and recovery procedures.

